m had  any problems or concerns with their  careers they were quickly moving up the  corporate ladder and likely earning fat  salaries but all this changed during  occupy wall street when one of their  mutual friends accused them of  profiteering from an unequal system the  duo didn't instantly quit their jobs or  anything but this statement stuck with  them and they started looking for ways  in which they could bridge the gap  between retail investors and wall street  one of the most obvious disparities they  stumbled upon were the lofty fees and  large minimum deposits required by most  brokerages now the fees weren't actually  that bad in nominal terms given that it  was just 50 cents or a dollar per share  or per option that you purchased but  from the back end it only cost pennies  to execute each of these trades so  brokerages were enjoying massive profit  margins combine this with the rise of  free-to-use games and apps like clash of  clans and facebook and it was clear to  vlad and baiju that they should create a  free to use brokerage to match the  underlying mission of the company they  selected the name robinhood which is of  course a reference to the disney  character that steals from the rich and  the gifts to the poor aside from free  trades and no minimum balance the dual  placed a massive emphasis on the app's  ui if the app was going to appeal to  regular people it better be easy to  navigate baiju says that his goal with  the app design was to make it quote dead  simple every time he came up with a new  feature or design he'd run over to  stanford from silicon valley to have  random college students test out the  future not only did this allow baiju to  develop an intuitive app but an app that  was fun to use you got confetti whenever  you bought new stocks and the buys you  paired this with clever vibrations and  haptic feedback that kept users engaged  robin hood would later face charges for  gamifying investments but this was great  for appealing to and retaining new users  and with that the duo was ready to  launch  robinhood while robin or dui was  phenomenal the key to actually  succeeding in the app market is  marketing whether that be organic or  paid baiju and vlad couldn't pay for  much marketing due to their limited  capital so they decided to spark  interest to another medium at  exclusivity people who wanted to use  robinhood were placed on a waiting list  and they could move up on the waiting  list by referring friends and the family  to robinhood not only did this make the  original launch easier to handle from  the back end thanks to a limited user  count but it gave people who made it  through the cut exclusivity which is  extremely powerful naturally as humans  we crave exclusivity because it gives us  a sense of belonging and importance as  well as a certain level of social clout  every luxury company out there leverages  this instinct to the max but likely the  best example of this within the tech  world is gmail back in 2004 when gmail  was launched google was the small player  going against giants like microsoft and  yahoo plus they had limited  infrastructure so they made a gmail  invite only and this is how it was till  2007. clearly this worked out extremely  well for gmail and the same could be  said about robinhood following their  initial launch in 2013 they were able to  build up a waiting list of 50 000 people  and by the time they launched on the  apple app store this number had  ballooned to 1 million people this  massive user interest allowed robinhood  to raise tens of millions from venture  capitalists and start investing in new  features and marketing in 2018 for  example they started rolling out the  infamous robin crypto and in late 2019  they launched fractional shares the new  funding also allowed them to start their  famous free stock referral program that  basically every finance youtuber has  milked to death in the meantime robin  hood was also upgrading their backend  infrastructure as well in late 2018 for  instance they started to self-clear  their orders all these new features made  robinhood more and more established  within the brokerage industry and that's  when the pandemic hit and the stock  market crashed generally this would be a  terrible time for brokerages but a lot  of retail traders were actually looking  to buy the crash combine this with  pop-up house money printer and it didn't  take long for stocks to recover and this  time tens of millions of retail traders  were on board it looked like things  couldn't get any better for robin hood  but in the background there were  actually several red flags  likely the biggest red flag surrounding  robin hood was the revenue model while  they weren't directly charging users for  trades they were charging users from the  backend through something called a  payment for order flow similar to google  and facebook collecting information on  our browsing habits and selling the data  to advertisers robinhood was collecting  all of our trades and selling them to  wall street firms called market makers  now robin hood didn't actually invent  this revenue model low-cost brokerages  like e-trade have been leveraging  payment for order flow for years but  this doesn't mean that it's a good  strategy payment for order flow is not  only heavily frowned upon but actually  illegal in several countries including  the uk canada and australia but wait a  minute what's the problem with payment  for order flow in the first place it  gives us basically unlimited free trades  right well the truth is it's not  actually free you're just charged  indirectly market makers aren't idiots  to pay for our emotional day trades and  hype investments they're paying for the  order flow to flip our orders and make a  profit for example let's say you're  willing to buy one share of apple for  100  if this order executes that means the  market maker was able to find someone  that was willing to sell one share of  apple for 99.50 or even 99  this gives the market maker 50 cents or  one dollar worth of profit which they'll  split with robinhood the difference  between how much you're willing to pay  and how much the seller is going to sell  for is called a bid ask spread and  market makers have been leveraging such  disparities for decades usually this  isn't a problem because market makers  only make a penny or two from each share  but given that market makers working  with robin hood have to split their  profits with robin hood their bid-ask  spreads are generally way larger in fact  the wall street journal found that  robin's spreads were as much as 60 times  larger than charles schwab so with the  robin hood sure you don't have to pay 50  cents for every share you purchase up  front but you're constantly overpaying  for every share you purchase which  indirectly charges you around the same  amount if not more and to make things  worse with traditional brokerages since  you're paying the fee you're the  customer and you're the top priority for  the brokerage but with robinhood since  the market makers are the ones that are  technically paying the fees even though  you're the one being charged for it  market makers are the customer and  they're the top priority  robin hood's broken revenue model would  eventually come back to haunt them but  in the late 2010s they had much bigger  issues to address in july of 2019 for  example robin hood admitted that they  were throwing the user's passwords in a  readable form across their servers which  had made it easy for hackers to steal  passwords and subsequently steal money  from these accounts similarly just a few  months ago robinhood confirmed that a  voice phishing scheme allowed hackers to  steal the email addresses of 5 million  customers and the full names of 2  million customers aside from these  security breaches a couple of wall  street bets users had found a glitch  within robinhood that allowed them to  borrow an infinite amount of money the  glitch entailed purchasing shares on  margin and then selling covered calls on  these shares users could then use the  proceeds from selling covered calls to  purchase more stock and sell more  covered calls on these newly purchased  shares and the cycle continues many wall  street bets users used this glitch to  engage in extremely risky trading which  resulted in many accounts sustaining six  figure losses and when all the fun was  over robin hood was the one that had to  pay the bill but none of these issues  hold a candle to the pr nightmare that  followed the suicide of alexander kearns  alexander was a university of nebraska  student that had been margin trading on  robinhood this wasn't going too well  though and at one point robinhood said  that his account balance was negative  730 thousand dollars after seeing this  alexander took his own life and on a  suicide note he accused robin hood of  allowing him to take on too much risk  the worst part about this was that the  negative 730 000 account balance wasn't  even legitimate it was a temporary  settlement glitch that would have  resolved by itself as you would guess  this led to several lawsuits regarding  gamification and risk disclosures many  of which are still ongoing but all these  lawsuits were just a sneak peek into  what would follow after the gamestop  fiasco  by now i'm sure all of y'all are  familiar with what happened with  gamestop wall street bets identified a  massive short interest in gamestop and  proceeded to buy heavily into the stock  it's not clear if this actually resulted  in a short squeeze or not but the hype  and momentum itself took gamestop to the  moon and robin hood was at the center of  this movement when things started  getting spicy though robin hood  restricted users from purchasing  gamestop and other short stocks their  official reasoning for this was that the  stocks were getting too volatile and  that they were trying to protect their  users from massive losses but given  their history with risk this was rather  hard to believe and suspicions went  elsewhere one of robinhood's biggest  market maker customers is a company  called citadel which also happened to  have a major short position in gamestop  now this isn't confirmed but it seems  like robin hood was pressured by citadel  to halt trading on gamestop and fearful  of losing one of their biggest customers  robinhood complied this is the problem  with their customers and users being  different people this trading halt was  followed up by dozens of class action  lawsuits but this was the least of  robin's problems whether the speculation  around robner's actions is true or not  the incident destroyed their reputation  as the broker of the people and the user  goat has suffered ever since despite  this poor outlook robin hood would  actually experience one last hurrah  accidentally in march april and may of  2021 dogecoin would rock it from just a  few cents up to 73 cents and given that  you could buy dogecoin on robin hood  this gave robinhood one more spectacular  quarter but after elon's appearance on  saturday night live dogecoin fell off a  cliff and sorted robinhood and robin  hood's initially hyped ipo ended up  being a massive bust  in terms of robinhood's short-term  future it's really not looking that  great  sentiment surrounding robin hood is  still as bad as ever and it's going to  be nearly impossible to beat the revenue  numbers from last year which largely  benefited from gamestop and dogecoin  fortunately robinhood has billions in  the bank and can weather a few painful  quarters or even a few painful years  maybe in the long term robin hood's  phenomenal ui will end up shining  through to retail investors but we'll  just have to wait and see at the end of  the day while robinhood did several  things right it seems like their entire  mission was a bit misguided you see i  don't think people hate wall street  because of high fees or 